---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import InfoBox from "../components/InfoBox.astro";
import Table from "../components/Table.astro";

const frontmatterFields = [
  [
    "<code>name</code>",
    "No",
    "Display name for the skill. Defaults to directory name.",
  ],
  [
    "<code>description</code>",
    "Recommended",
    "What the skill does. Claude uses this to decide when to apply it.",
  ],
  [
    "<code>allowed-tools</code>",
    "No",
    "Tools Claude can use without permission prompts when active.",
  ],
  [
    "<code>disable-model-invocation</code>",
    "No",
    "Set to <code>true</code> to prevent Claude from auto-loading this skill.",
  ],
  [
    "<code>user-invocable</code>",
    "No",
    "Set to <code>false</code> to hide from the <code>/</code> menu.",
  ],
  [
    "<code>context</code>",
    "No",
    "Set to <code>fork</code> to run in an isolated subagent.",
  ],
  [
    "<code>model</code>",
    "No",
    "Model to use when this skill is active.",
  ],
];

const invocationRows = [
  [
    "(default)",
    "Yes",
    "Yes",
    "Description always in context; full skill loads when invoked",
  ],
  [
    "<code>disable-model-invocation: true</code>",
    "Yes",
    "No",
    "Description not in context; loads only when you invoke",
  ],
  [
    "<code>user-invocable: false</code>",
    "No",
    "Yes",
    "Description always in context; loads when Claude invokes",
  ],
];
---

<Layout title="Restrict Tool Access — Claude Code Skills">
  <article>
    <div class="breadcrumb">
      <span>Docs</span>
      <span class="sep">/</span>
      <span>Skills</span>
      <span class="sep">/</span>
      <span class="current">Restrict Tool Access</span>
    </div>

    <h1>Restrict Tool Access</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
      Control which tools Claude can use when a skill is active using the
      <code>allowed-tools</code> frontmatter field. Build read-only modes,
      safe reviewers, and scoped automation.
    </p>

    <section id="overview">
      <h2>Overview</h2>
      <p>
        Skills extend what Claude can do in Claude Code. Each skill is defined
        by a <code>SKILL.md</code> file with YAML frontmatter and markdown
        instructions. The <code>allowed-tools</code> field lets you limit which
        tools Claude has access to when a skill is running, creating safer and
        more predictable workflows.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="basic-usage">
      <h2>Basic usage</h2>
      <p>
        Use the <code>allowed-tools</code> field in your skill's frontmatter to
        specify a comma-separated list of tools. This example creates a
        read-only mode where Claude can explore files but not modify them:
      </p>

      <CodeBlock lang="yaml" title="SKILL.md">
        {`---
name: safe-reader
description: Read files without making changes
allowed-tools: Read, Grep, Glob
---`}
      </CodeBlock>

      <p>
        When <code>safe-reader</code> is active, Claude can only use the
        <code>Read</code>, <code>Grep</code>, and <code>Glob</code> tools.
        Write, Edit, Bash, and all other tools are blocked for the duration
        of the skill.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="frontmatter-reference">
      <h2>Frontmatter reference</h2>
      <p>
        Skills are configured through YAML frontmatter at the top of
        <code>SKILL.md</code>. All fields are optional, but
        <code>description</code> is recommended so Claude knows when to use the
        skill.
      </p>

      <Table
        headers={["Field", "Required", "Description"]}
        rows={frontmatterFields}
      />

      <CodeBlock lang="yaml" title="Full frontmatter example">
        {`---
name: my-skill
description: What this skill does
disable-model-invocation: true
allowed-tools: Read, Grep, Glob
---

Your skill instructions here...`}
      </CodeBlock>
    </section>

    <section id="controlling-access">
      <h2>Three ways to control skill access</h2>
      <p>
        Beyond <code>allowed-tools</code>, there are additional ways to control
        which skills Claude can invoke and how they behave:
      </p>

      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Disable all skills</h3>
          <p>
            Deny the <code>Skill</code> tool entirely in your permission
            settings:
          </p>
          <CodeBlock lang="text">
            {`# Add to deny rules:
Skill`}
          </CodeBlock>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h3>Allow or deny specific skills</h3>
          <p>Use permission rules to target individual skills:</p>
          <CodeBlock lang="text">
            {`# Allow only specific skills
Skill(commit)
Skill(review-pr *)

# Deny specific skills
Skill(deploy *)`}
          </CodeBlock>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h3>Hide individual skills</h3>
          <p>
            Add <code>disable-model-invocation: true</code> to frontmatter.
            This removes the skill from Claude's context entirely.
          </p>
          <CodeBlock lang="yaml">
            {`---
name: deploy
disable-model-invocation: true
---`}
          </CodeBlock>
        </div>
      </div>

      <InfoBox type="note">
        Permission syntax: <code>Skill(name)</code> for exact match,
        <code>Skill(name *)</code> for prefix match with any arguments.
      </InfoBox>
    </section>

    <section id="invocation-control">
      <h2>Invocation control</h2>
      <p>
        Two frontmatter fields control who can trigger a skill and whether
        it appears in context:
      </p>

      <Table
        headers={["Frontmatter", "You can invoke", "Claude can invoke", "When loaded"]}
        rows={invocationRows}
      />

      <InfoBox type="tip">
        Use <code>disable-model-invocation: true</code> for workflows with
        side effects like deploys or notifications. You don't want Claude
        deciding to deploy because your code looks ready.
      </InfoBox>
    </section>

    <section id="practical-examples">
      <h2>Practical examples</h2>

      <h3>Code reviewer (read-only)</h3>
      <p>A skill that reviews code without making changes:</p>
      <CodeBlock lang="yaml" title=".claude/skills/review/SKILL.md">
        {`---
name: review
description: Review code for issues and suggest improvements
allowed-tools: Read, Grep, Glob
---

Review the code and provide feedback on:
1. Potential bugs or logic errors
2. Performance concerns
3. Security issues
4. Code style and readability

Do NOT make any changes. Only provide suggestions.`}
      </CodeBlock>

      <h3>Deploy workflow (manual only)</h3>
      <p>A skill that only you can trigger, with scoped tool access:</p>
      <CodeBlock lang="yaml" title=".claude/skills/deploy/SKILL.md">
        {`---
name: deploy
description: Deploy the application to production
disable-model-invocation: true
allowed-tools: Bash(npm run build), Bash(npm run test), Bash(git *)
---

Deploy $ARGUMENTS to production:
1. Run the test suite
2. Build the application
3. Push to the deployment target
4. Verify the deployment succeeded`}
      </CodeBlock>

      <h3>Research agent (forked context)</h3>
      <p>A skill that runs in an isolated subagent for deep research:</p>
      <CodeBlock lang="yaml" title=".claude/skills/deep-research/SKILL.md">
        {`---
name: deep-research
description: Research a topic thoroughly
context: fork
agent: Explore
---

Research $ARGUMENTS thoroughly:
1. Find relevant files using Glob and Grep
2. Read and analyze the code
3. Summarize findings with specific file references`}
      </CodeBlock>
    </section>

    <section id="skill-arguments">
      <h2>Passing arguments</h2>
      <p>
        Skills accept arguments via the <code>$ARGUMENTS</code> placeholder.
        Individual arguments are available by index with
        <code>$ARGUMENTS[N]</code> or the shorthand <code>$N</code>:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock lang="yaml" title=".claude/skills/migrate/SKILL.md">
        {`---
name: migrate-component
description: Migrate a component between frameworks
---

Migrate the $0 component from $1 to $2.
Preserve all existing behavior and tests.`}
      </CodeBlock>
      <p>
        Running <code>/migrate-component SearchBar React Vue</code> replaces
        <code>$0</code> with <code>SearchBar</code>, <code>$1</code> with
        <code>React</code>, and <code>$2</code> with <code>Vue</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="where-skills-live">
      <h2>Where skills live</h2>
      <p>
        Where you store a skill determines its scope. Higher-priority locations
        win when names conflict:
      </p>
      <div class="scope-list">
        <div class="scope-item">
          <span class="scope-name">Enterprise</span>
          <span class="scope-path">Managed settings</span>
          <span class="scope-applies">All users in your org</span>
        </div>
        <div class="scope-item">
          <span class="scope-name">Personal</span>
          <span class="scope-path">~/.claude/skills/&lt;name&gt;/SKILL.md</span>
          <span class="scope-applies">All your projects</span>
        </div>
        <div class="scope-item">
          <span class="scope-name">Project</span>
          <span class="scope-path">.claude/skills/&lt;name&gt;/SKILL.md</span>
          <span class="scope-applies">This project only</span>
        </div>
        <div class="scope-item">
          <span class="scope-name">Plugin</span>
          <span class="scope-path">&lt;plugin&gt;/skills/&lt;name&gt;/SKILL.md</span>
          <span class="scope-applies">Where plugin is enabled</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  article {
    max-width: 100%;
  }

  .breadcrumb {
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
  }

  .sep {
    opacity: 0.4;
  }

  .current {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: 2rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .subtitle {
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .subtitle code {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-family:
      "SF Mono", "Fira Code", monospace;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    padding: 0.1rem 0.35rem;
    background: var(--code-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  section {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.4rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  h3 {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0.5rem 0;
  }

  p {
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  p code,
  li code {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-family:
      "SF Mono", "Fira Code", monospace;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    padding: 0.1rem 0.35rem;
    background: var(--code-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  .card-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 1rem 0;
  }

  .card {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 1.25rem;
  }

  .card h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .card p {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .scope-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin: 1rem 0;
  }

  .scope-item {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 100px 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 6px;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
  }

  .scope-name {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .scope-path {
    font-family: "SF Mono", "Fira Code", monospace;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .scope-applies {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    text-align: right;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    h1 {
      font-size: 1.5rem;
    }

    .scope-item {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.25rem;
    }

    .scope-applies {
      text-align: left;
    }
  }
</style>
